The Security Council,
Recalling all its relevant resolutions on the situation in Yemen and the Red Sea, including resolutions 2216 (2015), 2722 (2024), 2739 (2024), 2768 (2025) and 2787 (2025),
Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, including the sovereign equality and territorial integrity of all States, and the obligation to refrain from the threat or use of force in international relations,
Reaffirming the importance of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea and of the freedom of navigation, as well as the rights and obligations of States under international law,
Expressing grave concern at the continued attacks by the Houthis against commercial and merchant vessels in the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden, including through the use of advanced weapons, which threaten maritime security, navigational rights, regional peace and stability and global supply chains,
Expressing serious concern at the negative impact of these attacks on the delivery of humanitarian assistance to Yemen and the region, on food security, and on the marine environment, including the potential risk of oil spills and other environmental damage,
Stressing the need for all parties to comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and to ensure safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need in Yemen,
Stressing also the need to address the root causes of heightened regional tensions, including the conflict in Gaza, and to achieve a comprehensive, just and lasting peace in the Middle East,
Underlining the importance of accurate, objective and timely reporting by the Secretary-General on the situation in the Red Sea, including on attacks and their impacts,
Bearing in mind the humanitarian situation in Yemen and the need to advance a Yemeni-led and Yemeni-owned political process under the auspices of the United Nations,
1. Decides to extend the reporting request in paragraph 10 of resolution 2722 (2024), as renewed by resolutions 2739 (2024), 2768 (2025) and 2787 (2025), for a further period of six months, until 15 July 2026;
2. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide the Security Council with monthly written reports on the situation in the Red Sea in accordance with paragraph 10 of resolution 2722 (2024), and to include in those reports, inter alia:
(a) Information on attacks by the Houthis against commercial and merchant vessels, including the use of advanced weapons;
(b) Information on the impact of such attacks on commercial shipping, freedom of navigation, global supply chains and regional economic stability;
(c) Information on the impact of such attacks on the delivery of humanitarian assistance to Yemen and the region;
(d) Information on the environmental impact of such attacks, including on the marine environment and the risk of oil spills;
(e) Information on the implementation of relevant Security Council resolutions and on any violations of international law;
(f) Information on the status of vessels and crews detained in connection with such attacks, including the MV Galaxy Leader;
3. Calls upon the Houthis to immediately cease all attacks against commercial and merchant vessels in the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden and to refrain from any further acts that endanger maritime security and navigational rights;
4. Demands the immediate release of the MV Galaxy Leader and all other vessels and crews that have been unlawfully detained;
5. Reaffirms the right of Member States to defend their vessels, in accordance with international law, from attacks, including those that threaten the safety and security of navigation;
6. Urges all parties to respect international humanitarian law, protect civilians and civilian objects, and facilitate safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need in Yemen and the region;
7. Emphasizes the need for a comprehensive political solution to the conflict in Yemen, including through a Yemeni-led and Yemeni-owned process under the auspices of the United Nations, and stresses the importance of addressing root causes of regional tensions;
8. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
