Bearing in mind the humanitarian situation in Yemen and the need to advance a Yemeni-led and Yemeni-owned political process under the auspices of the United Nations,
1. Decides to extend the reporting request in paragraph 10 of resolution 2722 (2024), as renewed by resolutions 2739 (2024), 2768 (2025) and 2787 (2025), for a further period of six months, until 15 July 2026;
2. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide the Security Council with monthly written reports on the situation in the Red Sea in accordance with paragraph 10 of resolution 2722 (2024), and to include in those reports, inter alia:
(a) Information on attacks by Ansar Allah (Houthis) against commercial and merchant vessels, including the use of advanced weapons;
(b) Information on the impact of such attacks on commercial shipping, freedom of navigation, global supply chains and regional economic stability;
(c) Information on the impact of such attacks on the delivery of humanitarian assistance to Yemen and the region;
(d) Information on the environmental impact of such attacks, including on the marine environment and the risk of oil spills;
(e) Information on the implementation of relevant Security Council resolutions, including the arms embargo imposed by resolution 2216 (2015), and on any violations of international law, including violations of the arms embargo and arms transfers to Ansar Allah (Houthis), and on actions taken by Member States in the Red Sea region and their compliance with international law, in particular respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of States;
(f) Information on the status of vessels and crews detained in connection with such attacks, including the MV Galaxy Leader;
(g) Information on all military activities and incidents affecting maritime security in the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden, including strikes by any State or coalition and their impact on civilians, civilian infrastructure, humanitarian access and regional stability, as well as information on the humanitarian and civilian impact of military operations in and around Yemen, including on civilian infrastructure;
3. Calls upon all Member States to fully implement the arms embargo imposed by resolution 2216 (2015), including by preventing the supply, sale or transfer of arms and related materiel, including advanced weapon systems, to Ansar Allah (Houthis), and stresses the importance of accountability for attacks against commercial and merchant vessels in the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden;
4. Condemns in the strongest terms the continued attacks by Ansar Allah (Houthis) against commercial and merchant vessels in the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden, and demands that Ansar Allah (Houthis) immediately and permanently cease all such attacks and refrain from any further acts that endanger maritime security and navigational rights;
5. Demands the immediate release of the MV Galaxy Leader and all other vessels and crews that have been unlawfully detained;
6. Reaffirms the inherent right of self-defence in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations and international law, and the right of Member States to take measures to protect their vessels from attacks, consistent with the Charter of the United Nations and international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea; underscores that nothing in this resolution authorizes the use of force or military action against Yemen or the territory of any State, or any action affecting the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Yemen, nor should it be interpreted as doing so; stresses that all measures taken to protect vessels must fully respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all States;
7. Calls upon all Member States to avoid any actions that could escalate tensions in the Red Sea and to respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Red Sea littoral States;
8. Reaffirms the sovereignty, political independence, unity and territorial integrity of Yemen, and calls for the cessation of attacks against Yemeni civilian infrastructure; stresses that Yemen's sovereignty, security and territorial integrity must be respected;
9. Urges all parties to respect international humanitarian law, protect civilians and civilian objects, and facilitate safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need in Yemen and the region;
10. Emphasizes the need for a comprehensive political solution to the conflict in Yemen, including through a Yemeni-led and Yemeni-owned process under the auspices of the United Nations, and stresses the importance of addressing root causes of regional tensions;
11. Calls for de-escalation and for diplomatic efforts to prevent further regional spillover, and stresses the need for the peaceful settlement of disputes in accordance with international law;
12. Calls for the full implementation of resolutions 2140 (2014) and 2216 (2015), including the Yemen sanctions regime;
13. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

